Cactus Code Thorn TestSubcyclingNoRestrict
Author(s)    : Liwei Ji <jiliwei.phys@gmail.com>
Maintainer(s): Liwei Ji <jiliwei.phys@gmail.com>
Licence      : LGPL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Purpose

Regression test for checkpoint recovery under subcycling of an evolved
group with restriction disabled (restrict="no").

Under subcycling, ODESolvers fills the coarse-fine ghosts of every evolved
group on a refined level by dense output from the level's source bands
(the parent's old state and RK stage derivatives), at every stage and at a
virtual end of each substep. On a level aligned with its parent, the
virtual-end value is the parent's RK update before its last
ODESolvers_PostStep. When the driver restricts a time-aligned window that
holds the finest level, ProlongateAfterRestriction overwrites these ghosts
on the levels aligned with their parents, by spatial prolongation from the
parent's final state. Under subcycling it does this for every evolved
group, restricted or not: restrict="no" only keeps the parent's interior
from being overwritten by fine data. Recovery prolongates every evolved
group on those levels, which repeats what evolution did, so a time-aligned
checkpoint needs no source bands.

This thorn is a trimmed copy of TestSubcyclingMC2 (scalar wave equation,
Gaussian initial data, fourth-order centred Laplacian, ODESolvers RK4,
three refinement levels) without the moving box or the added level. It
evolves the same wave twice:

  state    {u, rho}         restricted; error compares it with the
                            analytic solution
  state_nr {u_nr, rho_nr}   restrict="no"; ODESolvers_PostStep applies
                            u_nr = max(u_nr, u_floor) in the interior

The floor makes the parent's tl=0 differ from the dense output: with a
negative amplitude u decreases from zero, and once it reaches the floor
every RK update pushes it below again, so the floor clips it at every
step, also on the coarse cells under the finest level's coarse-fine
ghosts. If the driver left state_nr's ghosts at the dense output, the
recovered run, which prolongates them, would differ from the uninterrupted
run by far more than the testsuite tolerance. The floor is idempotent,
because ODESolvers_PostStep also runs at POSTRESTRICT and once more per
clock group at post_recover_variables.

2. Tests

  test/checkpoint-openpmd-midcycle.par
      3 levels, checkpoint at iteration 6, where the clocks are 2, 3/2
      and 3/2 coarse steps: the finest clock group [1,3) is restricted
      and level 2 is aligned with level 1, while level 1 lags level 0.
      The clocks differ, so the checkpoint carries the source bands that
      level 1's dense output needs. Runs on to iteration 8 as the
      uninterrupted baseline.
  test/recover-openpmd-midcycle.par
      Recovers that checkpoint and runs to iteration 8: recovery
      prolongates both groups into level 2's coarse-fine ghosts and
      replays level 1's dense output from the restored bands. Its output
      must equal the baseline's bit for bit.
  test/checkpoint-openpmd-aligned.par
      3 levels, checkpoint at iteration 4, where all levels are at 1
      coarse step: the single clock group [0,3) is restricted and levels
      1 and 2 are aligned with their parents. The time-aligned checkpoint
      carries no source bands. Runs on to iteration 6 as the
      uninterrupted baseline.
  test/recover-openpmd-aligned.par
      Recovers that checkpoint and runs to iteration 6; its output must
      equal the baseline's bit for bit.
  test/checkpoint-silo-aligned.par
      The time-aligned pair above with Silo checkpoints. The file holds
      no source bands. Runs on 1 rank (test.ccl), since a Silo checkpoint
      can only be recovered on the rank count it was written with.
  test/recover-silo-aligned.par
      Recovers that checkpoint (in relaxed mode, since Silo checkpoints
      do not carry BoxInBox's grid scalars) and runs to iteration 6; its
      output must equal the baseline's bit for bit.
